th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","history_only":true,"curation_sha256":"402117cd2ecc28eaf6387aaefcadec5f0c0540f0912f8a96983845daf37ec8e2","concept_id":"Year-2/1a-Math-V-Advanced-Linear-Algebra/characteristic-polynomial-difference-equation.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-2/1a-Math-V-Advanced-Linear-Algebra/characteristic-polynomial-difference-equation.html","questions":[{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_characteristic_polynomial_difference_equation_4d7c846e_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"The equation $x_{t+2}-3x_{t+1}+2x_t=0$, $t=0,1,2,\\dots$, has characteristic roots $1$ and $2$. Which sequence is the solution with $x_0=3$ and $x_1=5$?","options":["$x_t=2+2^t$","$x_t=1+2^{t+1}$","$x_t=3\\cdot2^t$","$x_t=5-2^{t+1}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4915d704b2c85500fa16ab46","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"no_diagnostic_value"},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_characteristic_polynomial_difference_equation_4d7c846e_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For $x_{t+2}-4x_{t+1}+4x_t=0$, $t=0,1,2,\\dots$, with the double characteristic root $2$, why are the sequences $c\\cdot2^t$ alone not enough to describe all solutions?","options":["$2^t$ does not solve it, since a double root yields no geometric solution.","Its solutions are not closed under addition, as $p$ has a repeated factor.","Each solution needs three initial values, as the root counts twice.","No $c\\cdot2^t$ meets $x_0=0$, $x_1=1$; that solution is $t\\,2^{t-1}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3751f20660a6da49a906ae96","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"no_diagnostic_value"},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_characteristic_polynomial_difference_equation_4d7c846e_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A real homogeneous equation has the nonreal characteristic roots $r=\\rho \\mathrm{e}^{\\mathrm{i}\\theta}$ and $\\bar r=\\rho \\mathrm{e}^{-\\mathrm{i}\\theta}$. Why is the real sequence $x_t=\\rho^t\\cos(\\theta t)$ a solution?","options":["It equals $(\\operatorname{Re}r)^t$, the real part raised to the power $t$.","It equals $\\tfrac{1}{2}(r^t+\\bar r^{\\,t})$, a combination of two solutions.","It is periodic, and periodic sequences solve such equations.","It equals $\\rho^t$, since $\\cos(\\theta t)$ is a bounded factor."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2533231e15a7cf80ffa63737","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_characteristic_polynomial_difference_equation_4d7c846e_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"For $x_{t+2}-5x_{t+1}+6x_t=4$, $t=0,1,2,\\dots$, with characteristic polynomial $(r-2)(r-3)$, which sequence is the solution with $x_0=3$ and $x_1=4$?","options":["$x_t=2+2^t$","$x_t=4-3\\cdot2^t+2\\cdot3^t$","$x_t=5\\cdot2^t-2\\cdot3^t$","$x_t=2+3^t$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"af2f1b24f24593968f6cef88","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"}]}}